---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="The Severed Link — Daf Yomi Game: Chullin 72"
  description="Forge four chains of Talmudic reasoning from Chullin 72 — the midwife's decree, the dueling verses, the emerged foreleg, and the hidden place — link by link, in the smithy of Pumbedita."
>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;700;900&display=swap"
  />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#0b0d11] !text-slate-100">
    <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
    <div class="border-b border-orange-900/25 bg-gradient-to-b from-orange-950/25 to-transparent px-4 pt-10 pb-8 text-center">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] !text-orange-500/80">
        <a href="/daf" class="hover:!text-orange-400">daf yomi arcade</a> · chullin 72 · 2026-07-11
      </p>
      <h1 class="frank text-4xl font-black tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">⛓️ The Severed Link</h1>
      <p class="frank mt-1 text-2xl !text-orange-200/90" dir="rtl" lang="he">כׇּל הָעוֹמֵד לַחְתּוֹךְ</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed !text-slate-400">
        On this daf, Rava admits: <em>"the scholars of Pumbedita know the reason"</em> — and the Gemara
        hammers out four chains of argument, ending on a limb that <em>stands to be cut</em>.
        <strong class="!text-slate-200">You are the apprentice in the forge-house of Pumbedita.</strong>
        Rebuild each sugya link by link: at every step, three bars of iron sit on the anvil —
        only one is the true next link. Strike well.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8">
      <!-- ============ Forge tally ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
        <div id="chain-badges" class="flex gap-2" aria-label="Progress: four chains"></div>
        <div class="text-right text-xs !text-slate-500">
          <span id="forge-label">⚒ 0 first-strike links</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="game-area">
        <!-- ============ Chain header ============ -->
        <div id="chain-header" class="mb-5 rounded-2xl border border-slate-800 bg-[#14181f] p-5">
          <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
            <span id="chain-kicker" class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-orange-500/90"></span>
            <span id="chain-heb" class="frank text-lg !text-orange-200/80" dir="rtl" lang="he"></span>
          </div>
          <h2 id="chain-title" class="frank mt-1 text-2xl font-bold !text-slate-100"></h2>
          <p id="chain-intro" class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-slate-400"></p>
        </div>

        <!-- ============ The forged chain so far ============ -->
        <ol id="forged-list" class="mb-5 space-y-0"></ol>

        <!-- ============ The anvil ============ -->
        <div id="anvil">
          <div class="anvil-card rounded-2xl p-5 sm:p-6">
            <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-slate-500">on the anvil</p>
            <p id="step-prompt" class="mt-2 text-[15px] font-semibold leading-relaxed !text-slate-100"></p>
          </div>
          <div id="choices" class="mt-4 space-y-3" role="group" aria-label="Choose the next link"></div>
        </div>

        <!-- ============ Chain complete ============ -->
        <div id="chain-done" class="hidden rounded-2xl border border-orange-800/40 bg-orange-950/20 p-6 text-center">
          <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-orange-400">chain complete</p>
          <p id="chain-done-line" class="frank mt-2 text-xl font-bold !text-slate-100"></p>
          <p id="chain-done-sub" class="mt-2 text-sm !text-slate-400"></p>
          <a id="chain-done-src" href="#" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-3 inline-block text-xs !text-orange-400 hover:!text-orange-300 hover:underline"></a>
          <div class="mt-4">
            <button id="btn-next-chain" class="rounded-xl bg-orange-600 px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-bold !text-slate-950 transition-colors hover:bg-orange-500 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-orange-400"></button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ============ The severed coda (after chain 4) ============ -->
        <div id="coda" class="hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700 bg-[#14181f] p-6 text-center">
          <svg width="64" height="88" viewBox="0 0 48 66" aria-hidden="true" class="mx-auto">
            <rect x="14" y="2" width="20" height="30" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="#98a2b3" stroke-width="5"/>
            <path d="M 24 38 a 10 12 0 0 0 -10 12 a 10 12 0 0 0 10 12 m 10 -7 a 12 12 0 0 0 0 -10" fill="none" stroke="#e0862f" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          </svg>
          <p class="frank mt-3 text-2xl font-bold !text-slate-100" dir="rtl" lang="he">וְכׇל הָעוֹמֵד לַחְתּוֹךְ —</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-slate-400">
            Here the daf ends. Mid-sentence. The hammer is raised and the page is cut:
            the last three words, <span class="frank !text-orange-300" dir="rtl" lang="he">כְּחָתוּךְ דָּמֵי</span>
            <em>("is regarded as already cut")</em>, wait at the top of tomorrow's daf, Chullin 73a.
            A daf about severed connections, severed by the printer — the daf's own chain stands to be cut.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-4">
            <button id="btn-end" class="rounded-xl bg-orange-600 px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-bold !text-slate-950 transition-colors hover:bg-orange-500 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-orange-400">See your forge record →</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ End screen ============ -->
      <div id="end-screen" class="hidden text-center">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-orange-800/40 bg-gradient-to-b from-orange-950/30 to-[#14181f] p-8">
          <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-orange-400/90">Your forge record</p>
          <div id="final-score" class="frank mt-2 text-6xl font-black !text-slate-100"></div>
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest !text-slate-500">links forged on the first strike</p>
          <div id="final-grade" class="frank mt-3 text-2xl font-bold !text-orange-300"></div>
          <p id="final-line" class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-md text-sm !text-slate-400"></p>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
          <button id="btn-replay" class="rounded-xl border border-slate-700 px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-bold !text-slate-200 transition-colors hover:border-orange-600 hover:!text-orange-300">↻ Reforge</button>
          <button id="btn-share" class="rounded-xl bg-orange-600 px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-bold !text-slate-950 transition-colors hover:bg-orange-500">Share score</button>
        </div>
        <div id="review-list" class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-left"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Learn the real daf ============ -->
      <div class="mt-14 border-t border-slate-800 pt-8">
        <h3 class="frank mb-3 text-xl font-bold !text-orange-200">Learn the real daf</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed !text-slate-400">
          Every link above is straight from the Gemara. Chullin 72 opens by asking why the midwife of
          yesterday's mishna is impure at all (answer: a rabbinic decree, reported by Rav Yosef of
          Pumbedita in Shmuel's name); derives Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Akiva's dispute from the verses
          of Numbers 19; brings a new mishna about an animal in hard labor whose fetus extends a foreleg —
          where everything hangs on whether the knife of slaughter came before or after the severing knife;
          and closes by asking how the severed limb can defile the fetus at all, when they touched only in
          <span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">בֵּית הַסְּתָרִים</span> — a hidden place. Ulla and Ravina
          each forge an answer, and Ravina's principle is cut off by the end of the page itself.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-xs">
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.72a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-full border border-orange-800/50 bg-orange-950/30 px-3 py-1.5 !text-orange-300 hover:border-orange-600">Chullin 72 on Sefaria ↗</a>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-slate-800 px-3 py-1.5 !text-slate-400">Rashi ×46</span>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-slate-800 px-3 py-1.5 !text-slate-400">Steinsaltz ×26</span>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-slate-800 px-3 py-1.5 !text-slate-400">Rabbeinu Gershom ×16</span>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-slate-800 px-3 py-1.5 !text-slate-400">Tosafot ×10</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 text-xs !text-slate-600">
          Part of the <a href="/daf" class="underline decoration-slate-700 hover:!text-slate-400">Daf Yomi Arcade</a> —
          a new game from every daf. Texts and links via the Sefaria API.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style>
    .frank {
      font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
    }
    .anvil-card {
      background: linear-gradient(165deg, #1a1f28, #12161d 70%);
      border: 1px solid #2c333f;
      box-shadow:
        inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04),
        0 10px 30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
    }
    .choice-card {
      width: 100%;
      text-align: left;
      border-radius: 1rem;
      border: 2px solid #333b48;
      background: #14181f;
      padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
      transition: border-color 0.2s, background-color 0.2s, transform 0.2s;
    }
    .choice-card:hover:not(:disabled) {
      border-color: #e0862f;
      background: #1a1c1d;
    }
    .choice-card:focus-visible {
      outline: 2px solid #e0862f;
      outline-offset: 2px;
    }
    .choice-card.cracked {
      border-color: rgba(207, 91, 71, 0.7);
      background: rgba(207, 91, 71, 0.08);
      animation: crack-shake 0.35s ease-out;
    }
    .choice-card.forged-pick {
      border-color: #e0862f;
      background: rgba(224, 134, 47, 0.12);
    }
    .choice-card:disabled { cursor: default; }
    @keyframes crack-shake {
      0%, 100% { transform: translateX(0); }
      25% { transform: translateX(-5px); }
      55% { transform: translateX(4px); }
      80% { transform: translateX(-2px); }
    }
    @keyframes forge-in {
      from { transform: translateY(-10px); opacity: 0; }
      to { transform: translateY(0); opacity: 1; }
    }
    @keyframes cool-down {
      from { stroke: #ffb45e; filter: drop-shadow(0 0 6px rgba(255, 160, 60, 0.9)); }
      to { stroke: #98a2b3; filter: none; }
    }
    .forged-item { animation: forge-in 0.4s ease-out; }
    .forged-item svg .hot { animation: cool-down 1.6s ease-out forwards; }
    .kbd {
      display: inline-block;
      border: 1px solid currentColor;
      border-radius: 4px;
      padding: 0 4px;
      font-size: 9px;
      opacity: 0.6;
    }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .choice-card.cracked, .forged-item, .forged-item svg .hot { animation: none; }
      .forged-item svg .hot { stroke: #98a2b3; }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const SEFARIA = 'https://www.sefaria.org/';

    const CHAINS = [
      {
        title: "The Midwife's Decree",
        heb: 'חַיָּה טְמֵאָה — לָמָּה?',
        kicker: 'Chain 1 · Chullin 72a:1–5',
        url: 'Chullin.72a.1',
        intro:
          "Yesterday's mishna ruled that a midwife who touches a dead fetus inside the womb is impure for seven days. But the daf just taught that swallowed impurity does not escape — the fetus should be like the two swallowed rings. The Gemara opens today by demanding a reason.",
        doneLine: 'The decree is forged.',
        doneSub:
          'Not Torah law but a rabbinic decree — because the head may emerge and slip back unseen; the mother is spared because she senses her own body, and too pained to warn anyone.',
        steps: [
          {
            prompt:
              'The objection stands: the fetus is like a swallowed ring, yet the mishna says it defiles the midwife who touches it. Who answers first — and how?',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'Rabba: a fetus is different, since it is destined to emerge — a ring is not part of the body’s future.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'Shmuel: this impurity is not Torah law at all — it is a decree of the Scribes.',
                why: 'That ruling is coming — but it is not the first strike. Rabba tries a logical distinction first, and it must break before the Gemara reaches for Shmuel.',
              },
              {
                t: 'Rav Hoshaya: a woman senses her own body, so only the midwife was included.',
                why: 'A real line from this daf — but it answers a later question: why the mother herself was left out of the decree.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'Rabba',
              text: 'A fetus is different, since it will ultimately emerge.',
              he: 'שָׁאנֵי עוּבָּר, הוֹאִיל וְסוֹפוֹ לָצֵאת',
              ref: 'Chullin 72a:2',
              url: 'Chullin.72a.2',
            },
          },
          {
            prompt: 'Rava turns on Rabba’s distinction. What is his objection?',
            choices: [
              {
                t: '“A fetus is destined to emerge — and a swallowed ring is not destined to come out?” The ring exits eventually too; the distinction collapses.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'A fetus is not “swallowed” at all — a womb is not a stomach.',
                why: 'Rava does not attack the category; he attacks the distinction. The ring, too, has a סוֹף — it will come out in the end.',
              },
              {
                t: 'Rabba forgot the mishna in Mikvaot about the swallowed rings.',
                why: 'The Mikvaot rings were yesterday’s discussion. Today Rava’s blow is simpler: rings also emerge in the end.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'Rava',
              text: 'A fetus will emerge — and a ring will not? The distinction fails.',
              he: 'עוּבָּר סוֹפוֹ לָצֵאת, טַבַּעַת אֵין סוֹפָהּ לָצֵאת?',
              ref: 'Chullin 72a:2',
              url: 'Chullin.72a.2',
            },
          },
          {
            prompt:
              'Rava concedes the reason lies elsewhere: “The scholars of Pumbedita know the reason for this matter.” Who is the scholar of Pumbedita — and what does he report?',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'Rav Yosef — who reports in the name of Rav Yehuda, in the name of Shmuel: this impurity is not Torah law, but a decree of the Scribes.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'Abaye — who says the womb is an exposed area, not a hidden one, so ordinary contact-impurity applies.',
                why: 'Fabricated — and backwards: touch inside the womb is exactly the hidden contact this daf will wrestle with.',
              },
              {
                t: 'Rav Yosef — who says the mishna follows Rabbi Akiva alone, that a fetus in the womb defiles by Torah law.',
                why: 'Shmuel’s point is precisely the reverse: the mishna stands even according to Rabbi Yishmael, who holds the fetus in the womb is pure by Torah law.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'Rav Yosef ← Rav Yehuda ← Shmuel',
              text: 'The midwife’s impurity is not Torah law — it is a rabbinic decree, and holds even per Rabbi Yishmael.',
              he: 'טוּמְאָה זוֹ אֵינָהּ מִדִּבְרֵי תוֹרָה, אֶלָּא מִדִּבְרֵי סוֹפְרִים',
              ref: 'Chullin 72a:3',
              url: 'Chullin.72a.3',
            },
          },
          {
            prompt: 'A decree needs a reason. Rav Hoshaya supplies it. Why did the Sages make the midwife impure?',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'Lest the fetus extend its head outside the womb’s opening — once the head emerges it is as born and defiles by Torah law — and it slip back without the midwife noticing.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'Lest the midwife draw the fetus out and touch it in the open air.',
                why: 'Then she would know she was impure — no decree needed. The fear is precisely the case she cannot see.',
              },
              {
                t: 'Because one who regularly handles the dead was declared permanently impure by the Sages.',
                why: 'Invented. The decree is case-by-case, aimed at one invisible moment: the head that emerged and returned.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'Rav Hoshaya',
              text: 'A decree — lest the fetus extend its head out of the womb’s opening, and no one notice.',
              he: 'גְּזֵירָה שֶׁמָּא יוֹצִיא וָלָד רֹאשׁוֹ חוּץ לַפְּרוֹזְדוֹר',
              ref: 'Chullin 72a:4',
              url: 'Chullin.72a.4',
            },
          },
          {
            prompt:
              'Last hammer-blow: if so, decree on the mother too — she also cannot see! Why is she left pure?',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'A woman senses her own body — she would know if the head emerged. And though she should then tell the midwife, she is too consumed by the pain of labor to warn her.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'The mother is already impure with birth-impurity, so the decree would add nothing.',
                why: 'Birth-impurity begins with emergence — and here nothing has emerged. The Gemara’s answer is about knowledge: she senses her own body.',
              },
              {
                t: 'Because touching one’s own body can never defile.',
                why: 'Not the Gemara’s move. The answer is knowledge, not contact: she feels whether the head came out; the midwife cannot.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'The Gemara',
              text: 'A woman senses her own body — and is too pained by labor to warn the midwife.',
              he: 'אִשָּׁה מַרְגֶּשֶׁת בְּעַצְמָהּ... וְתֵימָא לַהּ לְחַיָּה! טְרִידָא',
              ref: 'Chullin 72a:5',
              url: 'Chullin.72a.5',
            },
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        title: 'Two Sages, One Scroll',
        heb: 'רַבִּי יִשְׁמָעֵאל וְרַבִּי עֲקִיבָא',
        kicker: 'Chain 2 · Chullin 72a:6–10',
        url: 'Chullin.72a.6',
        intro:
          'Shmuel’s ruling assumed a dispute: is one who touches a dead fetus in the womb impure by Torah law? Rabbi Yishmael says pure; Rabbi Akiva says impure. Now the Gemara forges each opinion from the verses of Numbers 19 — and every verse one sage spends, the other must re-earn elsewhere.',
        doneLine: 'The verses are divided.',
        doneSub:
          'Yishmael: “open field” excludes the hidden fetus; 19:13 gives the quarter-log of blood. Akiva: “open field” adds the grave cover and walls; 19:13 gives the fetus; Leviticus 21:11 lets blood of two corpses combine.',
        steps: [
          {
            prompt:
              '“And whoever in the open field touches one slain by the sword… shall be impure seven days” (Numbers 19:16). What does Rabbi Yishmael forge from “in the open field”?',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'Impurity strikes only where the corpse is exposed — excluding a dead fetus in the womb: one who touches it is pure by Torah law.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'Including the grave’s cover and its walls as sources of impurity.',
                why: 'That is Rabbi Akiva’s reading of this verse. Rabbi Yishmael learns the grave cover and walls by received tradition, not from any verse.',
              },
              {
                t: 'Excluding one who touches a corpse under a roof, indoors.',
                why: 'Invented — impurity under a roof (tent impurity) is the stronger case, not an excluded one.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'Rabbi Yishmael',
              text: '“In the open field” — to exclude a fetus in its mother’s womb: pure by Torah law.',
              he: '״עַל פְּנֵי הַשָּׂדֶה״ – לְהוֹצִיא עוּבָּר בִּמְעֵי אִשָּׁה',
              ref: 'Chullin 72a:6',
              url: 'Chullin.72a.6',
            },
          },
          {
            prompt:
              'Then from where does Rabbi Akiva learn that a fetus in the womb defiles by Torah law? Rabbi Oshaya answers.',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'From “whoever touches of a corpse, of the life of a person” (Numbers 19:13) — which corpse is inside the life of a person? A dead fetus in its mother’s womb.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'By received tradition, with no verse at all.',
                why: 'Tradition is Rabbi Yishmael’s source — for the grave cover and walls. Akiva forges the fetus from בְּנֶפֶשׁ.',
              },
              {
                t: 'From “in the open field” itself.',
                why: 'He already spent that verse — on the grave cover and the grave walls.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'Rabbi Akiva (per Rabbi Oshaya)',
              text: '“A corpse in the nefesh of a person” — the dead fetus inside its mother.',
              he: '״הַנּוֹגֵעַ בְּמֵת בְּנֶפֶשׁ״ – אֵיזֶהוּ מֵת שֶׁבְּנֶפֶשׁ שֶׁל אָדָם? זֶה עוּבָּר שֶׁבִּמְעֵי אִשָּׁה',
              ref: 'Chullin 72a:7',
              url: 'Chullin.72a.7',
            },
          },
          {
            prompt:
              'But Rabbi Yishmael also holds Numbers 19:13. What does he forge from “the life of a person that died”?',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'A quarter-log of blood from a corpse defiles like the corpse itself — for blood is the life, and a quarter-log is the least a person can live on.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'That only a whole corpse defiles, never a part of one.',
                why: 'The reverse of his derivation — he uses this verse to make even a small measure of blood defile.',
              },
              {
                t: 'That a dead fetus defiles its mother alone, and no one else.',
                why: 'Invented — per Rabbi Yishmael the fetus in the womb defiles no one by Torah law.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'Rabbi Yishmael',
              text: 'The verse gives the quarter-log of blood — the nefesh of a person — which defiles.',
              he: 'אֵיזֶהוּ נֶפֶשׁ שֶׁל אָדָם שֶׁמְּטַמֵּא? הֱוֵי אוֹמֵר זוֹ רְבִיעִית דָּם',
              ref: 'Chullin 72a:8',
              url: 'Chullin.72a.8',
            },
          },
          {
            prompt:
              'And Rabbi Akiva goes further with blood. From “he shall not come upon any nafshot met” (Leviticus 21:11) he forges —',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'Even a quarter-log of blood drawn from two corpses combines and defiles in a tent: “nafshot” is plural, “met” is singular — two people, one measure.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
