              ref: 'Chullin 72b:2',
              url: 'Chullin.72b.2',
            },
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        title: 'The Hidden Place',
        heb: 'בֵּית הַסְּתָרִים',
        kicker: 'Chain 4 · Gemara, Chullin 72b:8–14',
        url: 'Chullin.72b.8',
        intro:
          'The Gemara turns back on Rabbi Meir. He said the flesh became impure by touching the severed limb — but they touched at the seam where they had grown together: a בֵּית הַסְּתָרִים, a hidden place. And hidden contact does not defile. Is Rabbi Meir alone in thinking it does?',
        doneLine: 'The hidden seam is exposed.',
        doneSub:
          'Ulla: even Rabbi Yosei agrees impurity passes at the very moment of separation. Ravina: the foreleg stands to be cut — and whatever stands to be cut is as cut already.',
        steps: [
          {
            prompt:
              'לֵימָא רַבִּי מֵאִיר לְטַעְמֵיהּ — say Rabbi Meir follows his own view that hidden contact defiles. Which mishna of his shows it? (Kelim 27:10:)',
            choices: [
              {
                t: 'A garment of three-by-three handbreadths, impure with a zav’s treading, that was split: the pieces shed the treading-impurity — but keep the impurity of contact, from touching each other while the garment was whole.',
                ok: true,
              },
              {
                t: 'The pieces keep nothing at all unless the zav’s own skin touched the garment.',
                why: 'That is Rabbi Yosei’s objection — the very next link of this chain.',
              },
              {
                t: 'A garment that was split is pure in every respect.',
                why: 'Invented — Rabbi Meir’s whole point is that a lesser impurity, contact, survives the splitting.',
              },
            ],
            forged: {
              who: 'Rabbi Meir (Kelim 27:10)',
